<<~ kanawai "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119">>



<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Kāpae

A first-class ''tombstone that shadows the layers beneath it'' — a raised marker that defeats fall-through, distinct from //absent// (a missing record that falls through to the next layer down).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/etymology>>

!! Etymology

//Kāpae// (Hawaiian) — to set aside, to put off to one side, to exclude or reject; to brush away. The word carries deliberate placement, not loss: a hand moves the thing aside and holds it there. A kāpae stays where the hand set it; it does not vanish, and another hand may take it back down.

This carries the pattern exactly. A kāpae marker sits ''above'' the record it excludes, casting a shadow down the cascade. The record beneath survives untouched; the shadow only hides it from the surface view. Lower the marker and the record beneath surfaces again, whole.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/core-cut>>

!! The core cut — kāpae shadows, absent falls through

Two ways a title leaves the surface view of a layered cascade (recipe over bags, store stack, membership stack). They read identically at the surface and diverge completely beneath:

| | ''kāpae'' (raised tombstone) | ''absent'' (hard-remove / retract) |
|---|---|---|
| what sits in the layer | a deliberate exclusion marker | nothing — the record dropped entirely |
| effect on lower layers | ''shadows'' them (hides any copy beneath) | ''falls through'' to them (a lower copy surfaces) |
| the gesture that raises it | EVICT · CLEAR · DROP · DELETE | MOVE / promotion source retract |
| reversibility | ''toggleable'' — lower it to re-admit | one-way at this layer; re-add to restore |
| read intent | "exclude this, even if a copy lives below" | "this no longer lives //here//; let the cascade resolve" |

The cut MUST stay sharp. A gesture that ''relocates'' a record (MOVE, promotion `@working → canon`) RETRACTS the source to //absent//, never raises a kāpae — the source falls through and the canonical copy beneath surfaces (residency-model anti-pattern #3). A gesture that ''excludes'' a record (EVICT, CLEAR, DROP) raises a kāpae, never retracts to absent — the shadow defeats any lower-layer re-add. conflating the two re-introduces the OverlayFS / moby#783 resurrection bug: a deleted upper record falls through to a stale lower copy that the operator believed gone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/toggle>>

!! The refinement — kāpae raises and lowers

A kāpae carries a ''toggle'', not a permanence. The pattern names two deliberate moves, each an operator gesture:

* ''Raise the kāpae'' (put the tombstone //up//) — shadow / evict. The marker goes up and hides the layer beneath. ''Remove-wins under contention:'' a raised kāpae defeats a concurrent lower-layer re-add — the shadow holds even as a peer re-introduces a copy below it. This carries the resurrection-prevention guarantee.
* ''Lower the kāpae'' (take the tombstone //down//) — un-shadow / re-admit. A ''deliberate re-admission'', never a passive fall-through. Lowering the marker reveals the layer beneath as it stood; the operator chooses the moment, and an effect record audits the re-admission.

The asymmetry carries the load. Raising defeats a re-add (remove-wins); lowering takes an explicit gesture (no silent un-shadow). A record never resurrects by accident — it surfaces only when an absent gesture lets the cascade fall through, or when a hand lowers the kāpae that shadowed it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/bag-cascade-sense>>

!! Sense (a) — the bag-cascade / residency tombstone (WITNESSED)

In the recipe/bag cascade a title may hold residency in N bags; the recipe walks them in priority order to compose the live view (residency-model). A kāpae raised in a ''higher'' bag shadows every lower bag holding the same title — the surface view shows the title gone, even though canonical copies survive beneath.

This already lives in code:

* `MemoryTiddlerStore.tombstone(title)` marks `meta.deleted = true`; the record stays readable via `get()` but drops from `listVisible()` — the raised kāpae (witness: `packages/lararium-tw5/src/memory-store.ts`, "Tombstoned titles disappear from listVisible() but remain readable via get()").
* `MemoryTiddlerStore.remove(title)` drops the record entirely → `get()` returns null = //absent//, falls through to a lower bag (witness: same file, "HARD-remove … distinct from tombstone's kāpae hide").
* The residency action handler routes the two deliberately: `tombstoneIn()` for CLEAR / DROP / DELETE (kāpae hide, shadows lower bags), `removeIn()` for the MOVE source retract (absent, falls through) — witness: `packages/lararium-tw5/src/action-handler.ts` `executeMove` / `executeClear`, carrying the comment "kāpae shadows, absent falls through".
* The conflict surface lists raised kāpae as readable drift state (`listKapaeBags` returns hides — residency-model #conflict-resolution).

the bag-cascade sense stands witnessed in shipped code and named in residency-model anti-pattern #3 (Kāpae resurrection — "First-class `tombstone` op distinct from 'absent'").

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/membership-sense>>

!! Sense (b) — the membership / governance stack (PROPOSED)

The same pattern carries into membership and governance. Model a keeper-cabal or Circle as a ''stack'' whose deletes ride as kāpae — the way a TW5 recipe stacks bags, the membership grammar stacks admissions.

* A member ''evicted'' from a keeper-cabal / Circle raises a ''kāpae on the membership stack'' — a shadow over that member's standing, not a hole the next layer fills. The eviction shadows any lower-layer re-add (a peer that did not see the eviction, or a stale invite beneath).
* ''Remove-wins under partition'' carries the ''siege-safe'' eviction semantics: under a network partition two peers may disagree, and the raised kāpae wins the merge — a member the cabal evicted stays out even as a partitioned peer re-vouches. The shadow defeats the re-add deterministically, so an eviction never silently reverses when the partition heals.
* ''Re-admit = lower the kāpae'' — a deliberate governance gesture (a fresh vouch through the Voice house / Talk Story), never a passive fall-through. The cabal chooses to take the tombstone down; the membership beneath surfaces again, audited.

This rhymes the recipe-as-bag-stack: membership composes as a priority walk over admission layers, and a kāpae shadows the layers below.

''THE MARKER RIDES THE EDGE, NEVER THE THING'' (operator ruling). Every comparable system raises its tombstone over an ENTITY — Matrix over a user, the fediverse over an instance, a CRL over a certificate, KERI over an identifier. An entity holds ONE global standing, so a marker over it demands global agreement: hence delivery, hence consensus, hence the write-race that produced Matrix's state resets and the CRL family's undelivered negatives. ''One failure wearing four costumes.''

Raised over a ''relationship'' the same marker needs none of it. The shadow scopes to one edge, no third party needs the news, the blast radius equals that relation, and ''two edges tombstone concurrently WITHOUT racing'' — because they name different objects. The mutual case the literature leaves open (Keyhive names and defers it; p2panda calls it "still to be explored") then dissolves rather than resolving: A shadows the edge (B→group) while B shadows (A→group), ''both shadows hold, both stand aside'', and remove-wins needs no winner. The place lands in a LEGIBLE state — no authority stands — which ''surfaces a fork'' instead of diverging in silence. Surfacing belongs to the code; deciding which side stays legitimate belongs to the humans (#fork-as-exit, cabal-realm#the-unswept-corner).

the membership sense stands ''built at the edge grain'' (`edge-kapae`). Acts accrete, each keyed by edge, gesture and its own content id, so a raise and a lower written from one causal frontier both survive a merge. Each act cites its causal parents, and the fold reads the ''causal heads'' of an edge: the acts no other act cites. No version number, arrival order or clock ranks one head over another.

''ONLY A FRONTIER OF LOWERS LIFTS THE SHADOW.'' A lower that causally follows every raise takes the marker down. A raise beside a lower with no causal order between them reads //unsettled// and ''leaves the shadow UP'' (remove-wins): a lower never lifts a removal by racing it. The place shows the fork, and a later act that cites both heads settles it by a deliberate hand. A frontier that cites a parent the reader lacks, or holds an act whose id fails its own bytes, proves no lift, so the shadow stays up there too. Forged acts drop before the fold; an act counts only under a hand that holds the edge (#/edge-hands), and only when its id derives from its signed bytes. An edge no act names reads //absent// and carries no shadow.

''THE MARKERS NUMBER FOUR, NOT FIVE.'' `dyadId` derives from exactly (deviceDid, operatorDid), the two ends of a device delegation, so ''the dyad names that very relationship'' and the delegation takes no marker of its own. It takes a consult: the Binding Gate reads the dyad's shadow (#/reach).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/four-edges>>

!! The four marked edges

> ''House reading, marked as such; the operator may overturn it.'' The membership stack holds four relations that take a marker. A seat in a charter takes none: a seat leaves by a roll (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/nexus-lifecycle">>).

|! edge |! its two ends |! what names it |! the board that carries its acts |
| ''dyad'' | a device and a face | `dyadId`: the vessel key and the veil key | the fleet's edge board, read by one PersonaGroup |
| ''vouch'' | a voucher and a joiner, inside one realm | `vouchEdgeId`: realm, voucher, joiner | the realm's edge board, read by that realm's own members |
| ''member'' | a member and a realm | `memberEdgeId`: realm, member | the realm's edge board, read by that realm's own members |
| ''carriage'' | a presenter (an operator, a place, a hosted leaf) and a Nexus | the presenter's nym at that Nexus | the carriage board (revoke, uncarry) and the antigen (kapae), on the always-carried tier |

A vouch names one voucher's stake. A member edge names the joiner's whole relation to the realm, whoever vouched: a marker over it holds against a fresh vouch from a hand that never saw the removal. A member who leaves by their own hand signs a departure and raises no kapae (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>).

''Name the board and its scope before reading it'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter">>). An edge board holds acts signed by the edge's own holders. It holds no roster, and no read of it may claim a whole set.

* ''The fleet's edge board'' derives its address from the fleet root. It crosses to the face's own devices alone: the sibling channel and same-operator sync. A dyad act never rides a Nexus board, since a dyad shown as set aside tells a Nexus that one device left one person.
* ''The realm's edge board'' and the realm's vouch board derive their addresses from the realm's own id. They cross to a peer that holds standing in that realm, and to nobody else: a removal stays among the parties to the relation it ends (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter#/the-private-room">>). They never ride a Nexus board or the public shelf, and no walker and no faceless carrier reads them.
* ''The carriage board and the antigen'' ride the always-carried tier (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edge-hands>>

!! Who holds an edge ~ two hands, and a lower lifts only its own hand's raises

> ''House reading, marked as such; the operator may overturn it.'' A vouch edge answers to its voucher AND to a chartered quorum of the realm it crosses, never to one realm key.

Two hands act on an edge. The ''holder'' hand signs with the one key the edge's own id names. The ''quorum'' hand signs with the seat quorum of the charter the realm stands under, at the seal epoch the act names. The four chartered classes read at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/kahu#/seed-and-nexus">>.

|! edge |! who may raise |! who may lower |! chartered class |
| ''dyad'' | the fleet root that bound the device | the same root | ordinary: one signature |
| ''vouch'' | its voucher | its voucher | revocation: the threshold of the original act, one signature |
| ''vouch'' | the seat quorum | the seat quorum | high-impact |
| ''member'' | the seat quorum | the seat quorum | high-impact; no single key acts |
| ''carriage'' | the seat quorum | the seat quorum | high-impact (kapae and rolls take the quorum) |

* ''Each hand folds its own causal heads, and the shadow stands while either hand's reading stands.'' A lower takes the authority of the raise it lifts: a voucher's lower never lifts a quorum's raise, and a quorum's lower never returns a stake its voucher withdrew.
* ''A quorum act counts under the seated threshold of its own epoch.'' No edge act uses the emergency class. A shadow strikes no seat's signature: a seat leaves by a roll alone.
* ''A person signs.'' A raise or a lower changes who holds authority, so a seat never signs one (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands">>).
* ''At two seats the quorum means both.'' Neither founder removes the other by quorum. Each withdraws their own vouches, and the exit runs through a fork (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-unswept-corner">>).
* ''The dyad marker reaches as far as the root's custody.'' A device that holds the fleet root's key can lift its own shadow.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rolls>>

!! Rolls carry shadows

> ''House reading, marked as such; the operator may overturn it.'' A seal roll carries every shadow, and a lift may cite a raise from an earlier epoch through the roll's own anchors.

* ''Citation alone orders acts.'' An act cites its parents by content id, and a parent may root on any epoch. No scalar, clock or arrival order ranks.
* ''The epoch names a roster and ranks nothing.'' A quorum act counts under the roster of the seal epoch it names. A holder act carries an epoch and no reader checks it.
* ''Every roll writes an edge anchor.'' The quorum of the epoch the roll opens signs the closed epoch's id, the closed epoch's seated keys and threshold, the causal heads of the quorum acts that counted at the closed epoch, and the anchor that opened it. The closed keys never sign an anchor, since they may stand as the reason for the roll. The anchor follows the carriage board's roll anchor in shape and law (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>).
* ''A quorum act at an earlier epoch counts through the anchors.'' A chain of anchors must carry that epoch to the reader's charter head: each anchor counts under the roster it opens, each carries the key-set the charter lineage binds, and the next cites each. The act must also sit in the causal past of the anchor that closed its epoch. A retired key-set therefore mints nothing after its roll: no raise and no lift.
* ''An act that does not count drops.'' A counted act that cites a dropped or missing parent reads //unavailable//, and the shadow stands.
* ''A lift after many rolls'' roots on the head epoch, cites the old raise by id, and counts under the head's roster. The old raise counts through the anchors between.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/accretion>>

!! Acts only accrete ~ the reader keeps what it counted

A board holds documents that any writer may edit, so a hand can delete a raise from the board. ''Every vessel keeps each act and each anchor it has counted, in a ledger of its own that never crosses to another operator.'' A reader folds the board together with its ledger, and it writes a kept act back onto a board that lost it.

* A delete changes no reading at a vessel that once counted the raise, and the board regains the act at that vessel's next read.
* A vessel that never read the raise cannot tell a delete from a withheld act. Withholding buys a delay; it proves no lift.
* The ledger holds signed acts and nothing compiled about anyone. No signed board head and no per-writer chain stands beside it: the first would seat a keeper over the board, and the second would list the board's writers.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reach>>

!! A standing shadow reaches every gate

> ''House reading, marked as such; the operator may overturn it.'' A standing shadow drives the share verdict, the sync gate and a Keyhive revocation, and the sealed store's key roll follows that revocation.

''One rule serves every gate.'' A shadow stands over an edge that reads //held//, //unsettled//, //unavailable// or //rejected//. Only //withdrawn// lifts it, and //absent// never shadows. A gate that reads a standing shadow answers as though the relation never stood. ''The peer draws Mu'': the same answer a peer with no relation draws, with no refusal that names a cause (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/carry-contract#/kapae-the-antigen">>). The operator's own doors name the reading, a fork included.

|! gate |! dyad |! vouch |! member |! carriage |
| the fleet closure | leaves the fleet | | | |
| the socket sorter, at admission | never sorts same-operator; the device sorts by what else it proves | | | a kapae'd or revoked presenter sorts as nothing |
| the share verdict, per document | the sibling lane and full device sync close | | every lane onto the realm's documents and bags closes for that member | the antigen draws Mu |
| carriage serve | fleet blocks close | | the realm's blocks close for that member | sealed carriage closes |
| the browser's federation floor | the sibling lane closes | | | |
| `lares cabal join` | | the vouch leaves the crossing | the crossing refuses, whatever vouches | |
| realm standing and the realm's own boards | | that path to the seed breaks | no standing; the edge board and the vouch board close for that member | |
| a face-grant seat and the Binding Gate | no seat, no bind; a lease roll skips the dyad | | | |

''Realm standing reads as a live query'': a member stands in a realm while some path of standing vouches reaches the realm's seed and no shadow stands over the member edge. A voucher who withdraws leaves the joiner standing wherever another path holds.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/keys>>

!! The key side ~ keys never outrun standing

* ''A seat without standing gets revoked.'' A device whose dyad stands shadowed leaves its PersonaGroup. A member leaves the realm's key group when a shadow stands over the member edge, or when no path of standing vouches reaches the seed. An //unavailable// or //rejected// reading closes the gates like any other, and it moves a key only once a raise counts.
* ''Every counted raise owes one roll of the seat it names, whatever acts follow it.'' A vessel that reads a raise and its lift together still revokes the earlier seat. Where standing holds after the lift, the vessel then seats the party afresh. A lift therefore never leaves an earlier key epoch in place at one vessel and a later one at another.
* ''Forward only.'' The removed party keeps what it read. A bag's content key rolls at the revocation, a causal act and never a clock (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road">>).
* ''No coordinator.'' Every vessel that reads the shadow, holds revoke authority in that key group, and stands under no shadow of its own in that scope performs the revocation. Each acts once for each raise it counted and remembers that in its own ledger. A revocation that finds the party gone reads as done. Keyhive's removal converges across replicas (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/convergent-mesh#/two-revocation-modes">>).
* ''Standing with no seat stays safe, and every door shows it.'' Where a lift stands and no vessel that read it can seat the party, the party holds standing and no key until a fresh crossing.
* ''Mutual removal ends in a surfaced fork.'' Two parties may each revoke the other before either reads the other's raise. No rule here prevents that, and none picks a winner: both signed acts stay, both parties leave the shared key group, and each keeps every key and every byte it already held. A vessel that reads a shadow over its own edge mints no further key epoch in that group and shows the fork at every door. The relation continues only in a fresh realm with a fresh key epoch, by an explicit fork, unless the realm's charter named a recovery council (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-unswept-corner">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/re-admission>>

!! Re-admission ~ a lift restores standing, never keys

The hand that raised lowers, by an act that cites every raise head, and an effect record audits it. For a realm the quorum reaches that act through Talk Story. ''A lift restores standing alone.'' The seat that follows always stands fresh: a fresh Keyhive membership at a fresh key epoch. Earlier content keys never return by default, and a bag's past opens only where its writers declared that past shareable when they wrote it (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-the-code-holds>>

!! What the code holds

`lares cabal join` consults the shadow over every vouch in the realm, under the voucher's key, and names each vouch it left out with its reading. `lares edge kapae` and `un-kapae` write holder acts to a board keyed by the Nexus and report whether the shadow stands. The fleet closure takes a shadowed set and no built door calls it.

▢ ''OWED'': the quorum hand and the member edge; the edge anchor and the cross-epoch count; the ledger; the two edge boards' crossing lanes; the consult at the sorter, the share verdict, carriage serve, the browser floor, the face-grant seat and the Binding Gate; the revocation and the key roll behind it; the fork door; a live multi-keeper partition witness.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! Law (Kānāwai)

* A kāpae MUST shadow every layer beneath it in the cascade, hiding any lower copy from the surface view.
* A kāpae MUST stay distinct from absent: a tombstone shadows, a hard-remove falls through. An implementation MUST NOT collapse the two (residency-model anti-pattern #3).
* A relocation gesture (MOVE, promotion source) MUST retract to absent, never raise a kāpae — so the canonical copy beneath surfaces.
* An exclusion gesture (EVICT, CLEAR, DROP, DELETE) MUST raise a kāpae, never retract to absent — so a lower re-add cannot resurrect the excluded record.
* A raised kāpae MUST win remove-wins under contention / partition (the shadow defeats a concurrent lower-layer re-add).
* Lowering a kāpae MUST stay a deliberate gesture and MUST write an effect record; a record MUST NOT un-shadow silently.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

